Inserting glottal stops between words is always allowed.

> Does not {la uiski ui} otherwise become /lau,iskiu,i/, with confusing dip=
hthongs? Is that desirable?

That cannot happen. The stress is on the first syllable of "uiski": {la=20
uiski ui}. If you leave out the spaces, you get {lau=C4=97skiui}, which can=
=20
only fragment into la uiski ui because:
1) "lau=C4=97ski" can't be a single word, because the stress is on UIski, a=
nd=20
the la falls off due to tosmabru
2) the trailing "ui" can not be part of uiski because again the stress=20
makes everything after the next syllable fall off. Also, the u of "ui"=20
cannot be part of uiski (*"uiskiu") because that's not a legal word=20
(remember that it would have to be pronounced [wiskiw], which is=20
illegal. And the i couldn't possibly be seperate either, because [i]=20
alone doesn't constitute a legal lojban word, as Lojban words can't=20
begin in a vowel.

